{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>I am the Elohim of your father.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it is as if it said 'the Elohim of your fathers,' in the manner of what is written (Exodus 15:2) ״אלהי אבי וארוממנהו״ — whose sense is 'the Elohim of my fathers.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'the Elohim of your father' — this is Amram, and it is as if He said 'I am your Elohim,' but He wished to associate His name with the righteous man who had died, not with the living." "<b>And behold,</b> when Moses heard the sound of the call 'Moses, Moses,' he heard the likeness of his father's voice. And so they expounded in Tanḥuma: He revealed Himself to him in the voice of his father Amram. Moses rejoiced and said, 'My father Amram is alive!' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'I am the Elohim of your father' — 'I have come to you with enticement, so that you will not be afraid'; end quote. From here we learn that the prophets, at the time of their prophecy, are alarmed and recoil, and need enticement in order to be able to receive and bear the prophecy; as we likewise find with Samuel, to whom He revealed Himself in the voice of Eli." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'the Elohim of your father' — Adam the first man. For when Hevel offered a sacrifice, he gazed at the Shekhinah beyond what was fitting to grasp, and therefore incurred the death penalty. And from this is alluded to here: ״and Moses hid his face״ — for he feared to gaze at what he had already gazed upon, like a person who is ashamed of what has already befallen him (״and Moses hid his face, for he feared to gaze״). And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in the first chapter of Berakhot: (Exodus 33:20) ״And He said: you shall not be able to see My face.״ The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: 'When I wanted to, you did not want; now that you want, I do not want' — that is, when I wanted to, at the bush, you did not want, as it is written ״and Moses hid his face״; but now that you want and say ״show me now Your glory״ — ״you shall not be able to see My face.״ And R. Meir disagrees, for R. Meir said: as a reward for three, he merited three — as a reward for ״and he hid (ויסתר)״ he merited the radiance of his face (קלסתר פנים); as a reward for ״for he feared (כי ירא)״ he merited ״and they feared (וייראו) to come near him״; as a reward for ״to gaze (מהביט)״ he merited ״the vision (ותמונת) of HaShem he gazes upon (יביט).״ And the explanation of this is: as a reward for the fact that he now feared to gaze upon Elohim — which is the Shekhinah, which is the attribute of judgment and is called a speculum that does not shine — he merited that in the end he gazed in the speculum that shines, which is the attribute of mercy."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:42.434002577Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}